## 1. Introduction
During past decades, tremendous efforts have been devoted to developing novel energy conversion and storage devices [1–6]. Among them, lithium-oxygen batteries (LOBs) have attracted immense attention due to their ultrahigh theoretical energy density $(3500\mathrm{~Wh~kg^{-1}};$ . Theoretically, the reversible deposition (oxygen reduction reaction, ORR) and decomposition (oxygen evolution reaction, OER) of $\operatorname{Li}_{2}0_{2}$ $\begin{array}{r}{(2\mathrm{Li}^{+}+\mathsf{O}_{2}+2\mathrm{e}^{-}\leftrightarrow\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathsf{O}_{2},}\end{array}$ $2.96\mathrm{V}$ vs $\mathrm{Li}/\mathrm{Li}^{+})$ take place on the surface of positive electrode in Li- $.0_{2}$ batteries during discharge and charge process, respectively [7–8]. Currently, several key technical barriers keep it from practical application, including high overpotential, poor rate capability, low round-trip efficiency and inferior cycle stability, originating from the sluggish ORR/OER kinetics caused by the inherent insoluble and insulating properties of $\operatorname{Li}_{2}0_{2}$ in aprotic electrolytes [9]. Therefore, it is vital to solve the above problems to promote LOBs towards practical application.
The electrochemical performance of LOBs is largely governed by the crystallinity of $\mathrm{Li}_{2}{\sf O}_{2}$ [10]. According to pervious reports, largesize $\mathrm{Li}_{2}{\sf O}_{2}$ with good crystallinity can be obtained in LOBs via solution-mediated growth pathway when the adsorption strength of cathode catalysts towards $\mathrm{LiO}_{2}$ is weak [11,12]. However, the production of ${}^{1}0_{2}$ based on the disproportionation reaction during the solution-mediated pathway is unavoidable, which is identified as one of the main culprits for the degradation of organic electrolyte and the premature death of LOBs [13,14]. In addition, the transport channels of the reactants will be blocked and the cathode surfaces will be passivated due to the formation of large-size $\operatorname{Li}_{2}0_{2}$ , resulting in the poor contact between $\operatorname{Li}_{2}0_{2}$ and active sites and the increased interface impedance [15,16,17]. During the OER process, a high potential is required for the decomposition of large-size $\operatorname{Li}_{2}0_{2}$ which further results in severe degradation of electrolytes [18,19]. On the contrary, amorphous film-like $\operatorname{Li}_{2}0_{2}$ tends to be gained via the surface-adsorption growth pathway when the adsorption strength of cathode catalysts towards $\mathrm{LiO}_{2}$ is strong [12,20]. Deserved to be mentioned here, amorphous film-like $\operatorname{Li}_{2}0_{2}$ has been shown to possess the potential to improve the performance of LOBs on account of its excellent electrical conductivity due to the abundant Li vacancy [21–23]. Therefore, it is of significant importance to develop an universally applicable method to adjust the adsorption strength of cathode catalysts towards intermediates.
Recent works demonstrate that strain engineering is one of the most efficient strategies to tailor the adsorption strength of catalysts towards adsorbate [24–27]. For instance, Zhou et al. fabricated the NiFe hydroxide with intrinsic tensile strain by a facile mechanochemical method to enhance the adsorption strength of NiFe hydroxide towards oxygenated intermediates to boost the water oxidation [28]. Specifically, after the introduction of tensile strain, the ${\mathsf{d}}{\mathsf{\Omega}}$ -band center of metallic sites in NiFe hydroxide will inevitably upshift, pushing the antibonding states of the d orbital above the Fermi level, which leads to difficult electron occupation of antibonding states and enhanced adsorption strength of catalysts towards the intermediates [29,30]. However, the tensile strain cannot be introduced in a controllable way based on this method [31,32]. On the other hand, MOFs have caught intense attention in the field of electrocatalysis as they exhibit large surface area, intrinsic high porosity, isolated active site and high design flexibility [33–36]. Originated from the boundless periodic duplication and ordering of same groups, the tensile lattice strain can be controllably introduced into MOFs by a simple organic linker scission strategy [32]. In principle, synergistically coupling tensile lattice strain with MOFs as ‘‘heaven-made match” can thoroughly unlock its potential in improving the adsorption capability of the cathode catalyst towards $\operatorname{LiO}_{2}$ , and thus improving the performance of LOBs.
To this end, a series of NiFe-MOFs with varying degrees of tensile strain are synthesized and used as oxygen electrode catalysts to modulate the growth pathway and achieve the discharge products with amorphous film-like morphology, and thus enhancing the performance of LOBs. The experimental results and DFT calculation reveal the enhanced adsorption strength of MOFs with intrinsic tensile strain towards $\mathrm{LiO}_{2}$ can be attributed to the upshifted d-band center of metallic sites, which is favorable for pushing the antibonding states above the Fermi level and thus decreasing antibonding occupation. Eventually, the growth pathway of $\operatorname{Li}_{2}0_{2}$ is converted from the solvation-mediated pathway to the surface-adsorption pathway, causing amorphous film-like $\operatorname{Li}_{2}0_{2}$ as the discharge product. As a consequence, LOBs based on MOF with $2\%$ tensile strain exhibit ameliorative discharge capacity of $12317.4\mathrm{mAh}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ at $100\mathrm{mAg}^{-1}$ and excellent cyclability of 357 cycles. This work presents unique insights into the crucial role of tensile strain in regulating the growth pathway of $\operatorname{Li}_{2}0_{2}$ , and the design philosophy of electrocatalyst proposed in this work can be extended to other energy storge field.
## 2. Experimental section
## 2.1. Materials synthesis
Nickel (II) chloride hexahydrate $(99\%)$ , iron (III) chloride hexahydrate $(99\%)$ , terephthalic acid $(99\%)$ , benzoic acid $(99\%)$ , triethylamine $(99\%)$ , N, $N.$ -dimethylformamide $(99\%)$ were purchased from Shanghai Aladdin Biochemical Technology Co., Ltd. To synthesize pristine MOF, $32\mathrm{mlN}$ , $N$ -dimethylformamide, $2\mathrm{ml}$ ethanol and $2\mathrm{ml}$ deionized water were mixed firstly. Then, $0.75\mathrm{~mmol}$ terephthalic acid, $0.56\mathrm{mmol}$ nickel (II) chloride hexahydrate and $0.19\mathrm{mmol}$ iron (III) chloride hexahydrate were dissolved into the above solution. Afterwards, $0.8\mathrm{ml}$ triethylamine was promptly added into the mixture solution. Subsequently, the solution was stirred continuously for $10\mathrm{min}$ and then ultrasonically treated for $^{8\mathrm{h}}$ . Finally, the pristine MOFs were obtained after centrifugation, washing with ethanol ( $3\sim5$ times) and drying with freezedryer for $^{12\mathrm{h}}$ . The synthesis process of $1\%$ -TS MOF is similar with that of pristine MOF with the exception of that a mixture of $0.675\mathrm{~mmol}$ terephthalic acid and 0.075 mmol benzoic acid was used to replace the $0.75\mathrm{~mmol}$ terephthalic acid. The synthesis process of $2\%$ -TS MOF is similar with that of pristine MOF with the exception of that a mixture of $0.6\:\mathrm{mmol}$ terephthalic acid and $0.15\mathrm{~mmol}$ benzoic acid was used to replace the $0.75\mathrm{~mmol}$ terephthalic acid.
## 2.2. Materials characterization
The crystal structure of samples was detected by employing Xray diffraction (XRD, Ultima IV diffractometer, Rigaku). The morphologies of prepared materials were investigated by scanning electron microscopy (SEM, Hitachi SU8010) and transmission electron microscopy (TEM, JEOL JEM 2100F). $\mathsf{N}_{2}$ adsorption/desorption isotherms were recorded on Micromeritics ASAP 2460 at 77 K. The chemical composition and the oxidation states of as-prepared samples were investigated by X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS, Thermo Kalpha). The valence-band spectra were tested by using ultraviolet photoelectron spectroscopy (UPS, Thermo ESCALAB 250XI PHI5000 VersaProbe III). The UV–vis spectra were performed on an ultraviolet spectrophotometer (UV-2600).
## 2.3. Battery assembly and measurement
The slurry composing of $80\mathrm{wt\%}$ catalysts, $10\mathrm{wt\%}$ Super P and $10\mathrm{wt\%}$ polymer binders (poly (vinylidene fluoride) (PVDF)) was used to fabricated oxygen electrode. The as-prepared cathode, together with lithium foil anode, glass fiber separator, and $150\upmu\mathrm{L}$ $1\mathrm{M}$ LiTFSI in dimethyl sulfoxide (DMSO) electrolyte were assembled into the 2032 coin-type LOBs in the glove box filled with Ar ( $\mathrm{\ddot{H}}_{2}0<1$ ppm, $0_{2}<1$ ppm). The batteries were rested in pure oxygen for 6 h before testing. The galvanostatic charge/discharge tests were conducted by using multi-channel battery tester (LAND CT2001A). The current density and specific capacity during the discharge and charge process were evaluated based on the mass loading of catalysts $\cdot1\operatorname{mg}\mathbf{cm}^{-2}.$ ). The cyclic voltammetry (CV) measurements were performed on electrochemical workstation (BioLogic SP-150) in the voltage range of $2.0{-}4.5\:\mathrm{V}$ at a scan rate of $1\mathrm{mV}s^{-1}$ . The electrochemical impedance spectroscopy (EIS) tests were implemented at $5\mathrm{mV}$ AC voltage in the frequency range from $1000\mathrm{kHz}$ to $1\mathrm{mHz}$ by using the same electrochemical workstation.
## 2.4. Calculation detail
The first-principles were implemented to conduct all spinpolarization density functional theory (DFT) calculations within the generalized gradient approximation (GGA) using the PerdewBurke-Ernzerhof (PBE) formulation. The projected augmented wave (PAW) potentials were chosen to describe the ionic cores and the valence electrons were taken into account using a plane wave basis set with a kinetic energy cutoff of $450\mathrm{eV}$ . Partial occupancies of the Kohn-Sham orbitals were permitted employing the Gaussian smearing method with a width of $0.05\mathrm{eV}$ . The electronic energy was regarded as self-consistent when the energy change was smaller than $10^{-6}\mathrm{eV.}$ . A geometry optimization was regarded as convergent when the energy change was smaller than $0.04\mathrm{eV}\mathring{\mathsf{A}}^{-1}$ . The vacuum spacing in a direction perpendicular to the plane of the structure was $15{\mathring{\mathsf{A}}}.$ The Brillouin zone integration was conducted by utilizing $2\times2\times1$ Monkhorst-Pack k-point sampling for the structure. Finally, the adsorption energies $\left(\mathrm{E_{ads}}\right)$ were calculated as $\mathrm{E_{ads}=E_{a d/s u b}-E_{a d}-E_{s u b}},$ where $\mathrm{E_{ad/sub}}$ , $\mathrm{E_{ad}},$ and ${\tt E}_{\mathrm{sub}}$ are the total energies of the optimized adsorbate/substrate system, the adsorbate in the structure, and the clean substrate, respectively. The free energy was calculated employing the equation: $\mathsf{G}=\mathsf{E}$ $^+$ ZPE-TS, where G, E, ZPE and TS are the free energy, total energy from DFT calculations, zero-point energy and entropic contributions, respectively. The U correction had been calibrated as $3.68\mathrm{eV}$ and $3.42\mathrm{eV}$ for Ni and Fe atoms in our systems.
## 3. Results and discussion
The synthesis procedure of NiFe-MOFs is shown in (Fig. 1a). The organic ligands (terephthalic acid) are partially replaced by benzoic acid during ultrasonication process, generating NiFe-MOFs with organic linker scission, which weakens the interlayer interaction, and leads to interlayer expansion. Tensile strain can thus be modulated by governing the amount of benzoic acid in NiFe-MOFs. The successful introduction of tensile strain is firstly demonstrated by the high-resolution TEM images. As shown in (Fig. 1b), the lattice fringe parameter of the pristine NiFe-MOF is evaluated as $9.9{\mathring{\mathsf{A}}},$ which can be indexed to the interlayer spacing of the (200) crystal plane. As expected, with the partial replacement of terephthalic acid with benzoic acid during the synthesis process, the interlayer spacing of MOFs broadens to $10.0\mathring{\mathsf{A}}$ and $10.1\mathring{\mathsf{A}}$ when the molar ratio of terephthalic acid to benzoic acid is 9 and 4, respectively, representing the $1\%$ and $2\%$ tensile strain (abbreviated as $1\%$ -TS MOF and $2\%$ -TS MOF hereafter), respectively (Fig. 1c and d). The crystal structure of the as-prepared samples was characterized by XRD. As shown in (Fig. 1e), all the characteristic diffraction peaks of as-prepared samples are consistent with previously reported MOFs [32]. The enlarged XRD patterns (Fig. 1f) demonstrate that the diffraction peak of the pristine MOF at $8.9^{\circ}$ relating to the (2 0 0) crystal plane slightly shifts downwards to $8.8^{\circ}$ and $8.7^{\circ}$ for $1\%$ -TS MOF and $2\%$ -TS MOF, respectively, corresponding to the expansion of (20 0) interlayers, which agrees well with the result of the high-resolution TEM. Unfortunately, the crystal structure of MOF suffers a serious damage when the molar ratio of terephthalic acid to benzoic acid $\mathrm{i}s{<}4$ [32]. Subsequently, the SEM was performed to reveal the morphology of the as-prepared samples. The pristine MOF shows the ultrathin nanosheet morphology, which is capable of exposing abundant active sites (Figure S1a, Supporting Information). Such nanosheet morphology is preserved after introduction of tensile strain (Figure S1b and c, Supporting Information). The nanosheet morphology is further confirmed by TEM images (Figure S1d-f, Supporting Information). Additionally, the high-angle annular dark-field images and corresponding elemental mappings of as-prepared samples reveal that the elements of Ni, Fe, C, O are uniformly distributed over the nanosheet (Figure S2a-c, Supporting Information). Taking together, it can be concluded the successful synthesis of tensile strained MOFs nanosheets via a facile organic linker scission strategy.
The $\mathsf{N}_{2}$ adsorption/desorption method was employed to measure the Brunauer–Emmett–Teller (BET) surface area and the pore size distribution, both of which highly affect the electrochemical performance of LOBs [37,38]. The as-prepared samples share the similarly microporous structures according to the typical type-Ⅰ $\mathsf{N}_{2}$ adsorption/desorption isotherms (Figure S3a-c, Supporting Information). The pore size distribution diagrams (inset in Figure S3a-c, Supporting Information) confirm that all asprepared samples possess micropores. Impressively, the attendance of mesopores and macropores can be observed in $2\%$ -TS MOF, which is conducive to the diffusion of lithium ions and $0_{2}$ during reactions [39,40]. Furthermore, the BET surface area of the pristine MOF, $1\%$ -TS MOF and $2\%$ -TS MOF are calculated to be $\overline{{166.40\mathrm{m}^{2}\mathrm{~g}^{-1}}}$ , $199.25\mathrm{~m}^{2}\mathrm{~}\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ and $280.10\mathrm{m}^{2}\mathrm{~g}^{-1}$ , respectively. The increased BET surface area after introducing tensile strain is in favor of exposing abundant active sites, which is contributed to the improvement of the electrocatalytic performance [41].
The chemical composition and the oxidation states of asprepared samples were investigated by XPS measurements. The survey spectrum in Figure S4, Supporting Information, indicates the presence of Ni, Fe, C, H, O elements in the as-prepared materials. The Ni 2p and Fe 2p XPS core level spectra are displayed in (Fig. 2a and b), respectively. The two main peaks located at the regions of Ni $2{\tt p}_{3/2}$ $856.6\mathrm{eV})$ and Ni $2{\tt p}_{1/2}$ (874.1 eV) revel the existence of $\mathsf{N i}^{2+}$ , whereas the peaks at $862.2\ \mathrm{eV}$ and $879.3\mathrm{eV}$ are corresponded to satellite peaks [42]. Analogously, two peaks at $711.8\mathrm{eV}$ and $724.8\:\mathrm{eV}$ can be attributed to Fe $2\mathsf{p}_{3/2}$ and Fe $2{\tt p}_{1/2}$ , respectively, representing the existence of $\mathsf{F e}^{3+}$ [43]. Conspicuously, after the introduction of tensile strain, the characteristic peaks of $\mathsf{N i}^{2+}$ and $\mathsf{F e}^{3+}$ shift toward higher and lower binding energies, separately. The above results indicate the partial charge transfer from $\mathsf{N i}^{2+}$ to $\mathsf{F e}^{3+}$ , which can be attributed to the introduction of tensile strain leading to the charge unbalance on the metallic nodes. Thus the O between the Ni and Fe can act as an intermediate for keeping the charge balance, leading to the partial charge transfer from Ni to Fe nodes, and the stronger Ni-O-Fe interaction. As a result, the adsorption strength of catalysts towards oxygenated intermediates is enhanced to improve the oxygen-involved catalytic activity [32]. In addition, UPS valence-band spectra were employed to investigate the valence electron structure and probe the shift of d-band center [44]. As shown in (Figure S5a), the valence band maximum values are calculated to be $\mathsf{0.99e V}$ $0.40\mathrm{eV}$ and $0.20\mathrm{eV}$ for the pristine MOF, $1\%$ -TS MOF, and $2\%.$ -TS MOF, respectively, indicating the introduction of tensile strain brings the valence band and d-band center closer to Fermi level. The upshift of d-band center can be further confirmed by surface work function $\left(\varphi\right)$ . As shown in (Figure S5b-d), Supporting Information, the $\boldsymbol{\Phi}$ are assessed as $6.54\mathrm{eV}$ , $6.22\:\mathrm{eV}$ and $6.00\mathrm{eV}$ for the pristine MOF, $1\%$ -TS MOF and $2\%$ -TS MOF, respectively, indicating the $\boldsymbol{\Phi}$ of MOFs continually decreases with the introduction of tensile strain. It is worth pointing out that the decrease in $\boldsymbol{\Phi}$ means an increase in the Fermi energy level, as illustrated in (Fig. 2c) [45]. In addition, a smaller $\boldsymbol{\Phi}$ represents a weaker confinement of the electrons on the surface of catalysts, which is contributed to electrons escape form the surface of catalysts and their participation in the catalytic reaction. As mentioned above, the inherent interatomic distances are broadened and thus the tensile strain is introduced, resulting in the overlap of d-states between neighboring Ni and Fe atoms and narrowing of the d-band width. As a result, the d-band center of metallic sites moves upwards relative to the Fermi level in order to maintain the fixed d-states occupancy. As shown in (Fig. 2d), when intermediate $\mathrm{LiO}_{2}$ is adsorbed on the surface of catalysts, the coupling between the valence bands of oxygen in $\mathrm{LiO}_{2}$ and metallic sites in catalysts results in the formation of bonding and antibonding states. Subsequently, for the pristine MOF, the electrons fully occupy the bonding and antibonding states, resulting in a weak adsorption strength of the pristine MOF towards $\mathrm{LiO}_{2}$ . Analogously, for $1\%$ -TS MOF, although antibonding states present a slight increase in energy, it is still much lower than the Fermi level and thus can be fully occupied. By contrast, for $2\%$ -TS MOF, the upshifted d-band center of metallic sites pushes the antibonding states to a higher energy state, even above the Fermi level, leading to difficult electron occupation of antibonding states. As a result, the adsorption strength of $2\%$ -TS MOF towards $\mathrm{LiO}_{2}$ is enhanced, which is conducive to regulating the growth pathway of $\operatorname{Li}_{2}0_{2}$ , and correspondingly improving the performance of LOBs.
The Gibbs free energies (DG) at different potentials (U) on different surface are displayed in (Fig. 3e-g). For the pristine MOF (Fig. 3e), the $\Delta G$ from $0_{2}$ to $\mathrm{LiO}_{2}$ $\langle\Delta G_{\mathrm{step1}}\rangle$ is evaluated as $-0.46\mathrm{eV}$ , which is smaller than the $\Delta G$ from $\mathrm{LiO}_{2}$ t $\mathrm{\Delta\mathrm{Li_{2}O_{2}}(\Delta G_{s t e p2},-0.37e V)}$ at reversible potential $\mathrm{'U}=3.09\mathrm{V},$ ), indicating that $\mathrm{LiO}_{2}$ is stabilized on the pristine MOF surface [47]. As a result, $\mathrm{LiO}_{2}$ can suffer a dissolution and diffusion in electrolyte following the solution-mediated pathway to form discharged product $\operatorname{Li}_{2}0_{2}$ . In this circumstance, the spontaneous disproportionation from $\mathrm{LiO}_{2}$ to the $\operatorname{Li}_{2}0_{2}$ results in the formation of large-size crystalline $\operatorname{Li}_{2}0_{2}$ . A similar phenomenon can be observed in (Fig. 3f), which demonstrates the identical growth pathway of $\mathrm{Li}_{2}{\sf O}_{2}$ on $1\%$ -TS MOF surface as that on the pristine MOF surface. Interestingly, as the increase of the tensile strain to $2\%$ (Fig. $^{3}\mathbf{g}\rangle$ , the $\Delta G_{\mathrm{step1}}$ is calculated as $-0.01\ \mathrm{eV}$ , which is larger than $\Delta G_{\mathrm{step}2}$ $\left(-0.06\mathrm{eV}\right)$ at $\mathrm{U}=2.97\:\mathrm{V}$ , representing the unstable feature of $\mathrm{LiO}_{2}$ on $2\%$ -TS MOF surface [47]. Undoubtedly, the $\mathrm{LiO}_{2}$ inevitably accepts extra electron on $2\%$ -TS MOF surface to reduce to amorphous film-like $\operatorname{Li}_{2}0_{2}$ . The total overpotential is defined as $\Delta_{\mathrm{T}}=\mathrm{U}_{\mathrm{C}}-\mathrm{U}_{\mathrm{DC}}$ , where $\mathsf{U}_{\mathrm{DC}}$ and $\mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{C}}$ represent the discharge and charge voltage, respectively. Conspicuously, the $2\%$ -TS MOF exhibits the lower $\Delta_{\mathrm{T}}$ (0.62 V) compared with others $(1.07\mathrm{V}$ for the pristine MOF and $0.80\mathrm{V}$ for the $1\%$ -TS MOF), demonstrating the excellent electrocatalysis performance of $2\%$ -TS MOF towards oxygen electrode reactions.
Based on the above analysis, the pristine MOF and $2\%$ -TS MOF were fabricated as oxygen electrodes and assembled into LOBs to study their catalytic activity. The CV measurements are shown in (Fig. 4a). During the cathodic reduction region, $\boldsymbol{\mathrm{E}}_{1,\mathrm{c}1}$ represents the formation of adsorbed $\mathrm{LiO}_{2}$ $\mathrm{(LiO}_{2(\mathsf{a d s})},$ , which inevitably undergoes a secondary electron reduction process to from the amorphous $\operatorname{Li}_{2}0_{2}$ via the surface adsorption pathway $\ensuremath{\left(\mathrm{LiO}_{2(\mathrm{ads})}+\mathrm{e}^{-\mathrm{~}+}\right.}$ $\mathrm{Li}^{+}\to\mathrm{Li}_{2}0_{2}$ (s)). While $\mathbb{E}_{1,\mathrm{c}2}$ is closely related to the disproportionation of $\mathrm{LiO}_{2}$ , which is accompanied by the production of $^10_{2}$ ( $2\mathrm{LiO}_{2}$ $\mathbf{\Pi}(\mathbf{sol})\rightarrow\mathbf{Li}_{2}\mathbf{O}_{2}+\mathbf{x}^{3}\mathbf{O}_{2}+\mathbf{\Pi}(1\mathbf{-}\mathbf{X})^{1}\mathbf{O}_{2})$ , leading to grievous degradation of organic electrolyte, and deterioration of the performance of LOBs [48]. During the anodic oxidation process, the peaks located at $\mathsf{E}_{1}$ , a and $\mathtt{E}_{2,\mathtt{a}}$ are related to the removal of non-stoichiometric superoxide-like species (e.g. $\mathrm{Li}_{2-\mathrm{x}}\mathrm{O}_{2}\dot{.}$ $(\mathrm{Li}_{2-x}0_{2}\rightarrow(1-\mathrm{x})\mathrm{Li}^{+}+(1-\mathrm{x})\mathrm{e}^{-}$ $+\ \mathrm{LiO}_{2}\mathrm{.}$ . The $\mathrm{E}_{3,\mathrm{a}}$ at higher potential originates from the decomposition of tightly packed $\mathrm{Li}_{2}0_{2}$ film, which generally suffers a two-electron transfer electrochemical process $(\mathrm{Li}_{2}0_{2}\rightarrow2\mathrm{Li}^{+}~+$ $2\mathrm{e}^{-}+\mathrm{O}_{2})$ [49]. Significantly, $2\%$ -TS MOF based LOBs deliver the higher peak current and integral areas during both discharge and charge process, suggesting the favorable deposition and decomposition kinetics of $\mathrm{Li}_{2}{\sf O}_{2}$ on $2\%$ -TS MOF electrode. The oxygen electrode reaction kinetics are further investigated by EIS measurements. The charge transfer resistance $\left(\mathsf{R}_{\mathrm{ct}}\right)$ , which is closely related to the reaction kinetics, is represented by the semicircle at the middle frequencies in the Nyquist plots (Fig. 4b). Obviously, the $2\%$ -TS MOF exhibits the lower $\mathtt{R_{c t}}$ $\left(64.2\Omega\right)$ compared with that of the pristine MOF $(151.6\Omega)$ , demonstrating the favorable interfacial charge transfer kinetics on $2\%$ -TS MOF, which is conducive to enhancing electrocatalysis performance of tensile strained MOFs [50]. The ameliorative performance of LOBs is demonstrated by galvanostatic charge/discharge tests. (Fig. 4c) shows the charge–discharge curves of the pristine MOF and $2\%$ - TS MOF based LOBs at a current density of $100\mathrm{\mAg}^{-1}$ with the cutoff capacity of $1000\ \mathrm{mAh}\ \mathbf{g}^{-1}$ . The pristine MOF based LOBs exhibit a large overpotential of $1.11\mathrm{V}$ , causing a terrible energy loss. As expected, the overpotential of $2\%$ -TS MOF based LOBs significantly decreases to $_{0.61\mathrm{\V}}$ , which indicates the improved formation and decomposition kinetics of $\operatorname{Li}_{2}0_{2}$ . Subsequently, the full discharge curves at a current density of $100\mathrm{mAg}^{-1}$ with the limited voltage of $2.0\mathrm{V}$ are displayed in (Fig. 4d). Although the amorphous film-like $\operatorname{Li}_{2}0_{2}$ is considered to result in a small discharge capacity, $2\%$ -TS MOF based LOBs exhibit larger discharge capacity $(12317.4\mathrm{\mAh}\mathrm{\g}^{-1}\cdot$ compared to that of the pristine MOF $\mathsf{\cdot4424.2\ m A h\ g^{-1}}.$ ), which is originated from the larger BET surface area and the effective utilization of active sites for $2\%$ -TS MOF. Furthermore, the rate capability was examined at different current density with a cutoff capacity of $1000\ \mathrm{mAh}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}$ . As shown in Fig. 4e and Figure S9, Supporting Information, the overpotential of $2\%.$ -TS MOF based LOBs is lower than that of the pristine MOF based LOBs at all current densities. The round-trip efficiency of LOBs at different current densities are summarized in (Fig. 4f). Obviously, although the round-trip efficiency decreases with the increase of current density, $2\%$ -TS MOF always exhibits the higher round-trip efficiency at each current density. The above results indicate that the rate capability of LOBs based on $2\%$ -TS MOF is ameliorated compared with that based on the pristine MOF, which can be attributed to the unique pore structures and enhanced electron transfer on the surface of $2\%$ -TS MOF. In addition, the cycling ability of LOBs was evaluated at the current density of $100\mathrm{mAg}^{-1}$ with the cutoff capacity of $1000\mathrm{mAh}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ . As shown in (Fig. 4g), $2\%$ - TS MOF based LOBs deliver the remarkable cycling performance (357 cycles), which is about two times longer than that of the pristine MOF based LOBs (157 cycles).
The electrochemical performance described above is closely connected with the structure and morphology of discharge products. The XRD measurement was first used to investigate the crystal structure of discharge products. As illustrated in (Fig. 5a), the diffraction peak at $32.9^{\circ}$ corresponding to the (100) crystal plane of $\operatorname{Li}_{2}0_{2}$ could be explicitly observed for the discharged pristine MOF electrode, which indicates the high crystallinity of $\mathrm{Li}_{2}{\sf O}_{2}$ on the discharged pristine MOF electrode surface. Nevertheless, no significant diffraction peak could be detected for the discharged $2\%$ -TS MOF electrode. After the discharged $2\%$ -TS MOF electrode undergoing an annealing process at $120^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for overnight under Ar atmosphere, the diffraction peaks corresponding to $\operatorname{Li}_{2}0_{2}$ emerge, revealing the amorphous properties of $\mathrm{Li}_{2}{\sf O}_{2}$ formed on the $2\%$ -TS MOF electrode during discharge process [22]. According to pervious reports, the crystallinity of $\operatorname{Li}_{2}0_{2}$ is closely related to its morphology [51], and thus, the ex-situ SEM measurement was employed to further study the crystallinity of $\operatorname{Li}_{2}0_{2}$ . The irregular distribution of large-sized $\operatorname{Li}_{2}0_{2}$ can be observed on the surface of pristine MOF electrode, indicating well crystallinity of $\mathrm{Li}_{2}{\sf O}_{2}$ (Fig. 5b). In contrast, the film-like $\mathrm{Li}_{2}{\sf O}_{2}$ is uniformly and tightly deposited on the surface of $2\%$ -TS MOF, representing the amorphous feature of $\mathrm{Li}_{2}{\sf O}_{2}$ (Fig. 5c). However, with the continuous increase of discharge capacity, $\operatorname{Li}_{2}0_{2}$ grains with well crystallinity can be observed on the film-like $\mathrm{Li}_{2}0_{2}$ (Figure S10, Supporting Information). This is because the surface of $2\%$ -TS MOF is completely covered by amorphous film-like $\operatorname{Li}_{2}0_{2}$ , resulting in the variation of the properties of reaction interface, and thus, the growth pathway of $\mathrm{Li}_{2}{\sf O}_{2}$ is transformed from surface-adsorption growth pathway to solution-mediated growth pathway [52].
The ex-situ high-resolution XPS measurement was carried out on discharged electrodes to further reveal the characteristic of discharge products. For the Li 1 s spectra (Fig. 5d), the peak located at $55.3\mathrm{eV}$ is related to the deposition of $\mathrm{Li}_{2}{\sf O}_{2}$ on the cathode surface, while the peak at $56.1\mathrm{eV}$ is due to the formation of Li2- $\phantom{+}_{\mathrm{x}}0_{2}$ [53]. Thus, $\operatorname{Li}_{2}0_{2}$ acts as the main discharge product on the surface of pristine MOF cathode. In addition, small amounts of ${\mathrm{Li}}_{2-\mathrm{x}}0_{2}$ can be detected, which is situated outside of $\mathrm{Li}_{2}{\sf O}_{2}$ grain [54]. By contrast, originated from the low Li vacancy formation energy of amorphous $\operatorname{Li}_{2}0_{2}$ , the ratio of ${\mathrm{Li}}_{2-\mathrm{x}}{\mathrm{O}}_{2}$ to $\operatorname{Li}_{2}0_{2}$ is greatly increased on the surface of $2\%$ -TS MOF electrode, which is in favor of reducing charge overpotential, due to the preferential decomposition of Li2- $\phantom{+}_{\cdot\mathbf{x}}0_{2}$ . For the C 1 s spectra (Fig. 5e), four peaks can be clearly observed for both electrodes, which are corresponding to the ${\mathsf{C}}{\mathrm{-}}{\mathsf{C}}/{\mathsf{C}}{\mathrm{=}}{\mathsf{C}}$ (284.1 eV), CAOAC $(285.9\mathrm{eV})$ , $\mathtt{C-O=C}(288.0\mathrm{eV})$ and $\mathsf{C O}_{3}^{2-}$ (290.1 eV) [55]. Remarkably, compared with the surface of pristine MOF cathode, the spectrum signal of ${\mathsf{C}}{\mathsf{-}}{\mathsf{O}}{\mathsf{=}}{\mathsf{O}}$ and $\mathsf{C O}_{3}^{2-}$ originated from the side products is relatively weaker on the surface of $2\%$ -TS MOF electrode, indicating that the parasitic reactions are suppressed on $2\%$ -TS MOF due to the avoidance of the formation of $^10_{2}$ during the secondary singleelectron reduction of $\mathrm{LiO}_{2}$ on $2\%$ -TS MOF.
Furthermore, $\mathrm{Li}_{2}{\sf O}_{2}$ formed on different cathodes during the discharge process is quantitatively examined by chemical titration (the detail in Supporting Information, Note 1). The UV–vis spectra of $\mathrm{TiOSO_{4}}$ solutions immersed with discharge electrodes at different capacities are displayed in Figure S11a-c, Supporting Information. The corresponding Lambert Beer relationship (Figure S11d-f, Supporting Information) was used to assess the amount of $\mathrm{Li}_{2}{\sf O}_{2}$ formed at different discharge capacities. As illustrated in Fig. 5f, Table S1 and S2, Supporting Information, the amount of $\mathrm{Li}_{2}0_{2}$ formed on $2\%$ -TS MOF electrode is about $80.0\%$ of its theoretical value, which is higher than that on the pristine MOF electrode $70.9\%$ of the theoretical value), representing the parasitic reaction is restrained on $2\%$ -TS MOF electrode. Interestingly, with the increase of discharge capacity, the amount of $\operatorname{Li}_{2}0_{2}$ formed on $2\%$ -TS MOF electrode gradually decreases, which can be attributed to the gradual emergence of parasitic reaction.
Combining the above experimental results and DFT calculations, the rational growth pathway of $\operatorname{Li}_{2}0_{2}$ on the surface of different catalysts is clarified. Initially, during the discharge process, oxygen suffers a one-electron reduction reaction on the electrode surface to form unstable intermediate $\mathrm{LiO}_{2}$ $(0_{2}+\mathrm{e}^{-}+\mathrm{Li}^{+}\rightarrow\mathrm{LiO}_{2})$ Originated from the distinct surface properties of the pristine MOF and $2\%.$ -TS MOF, the subsequent reduction process exhibits the differences. Due to the relative weak binding interactions between $\mathrm{LiO}_{2}$ and the active sites, the pivotal intermediate $\mathrm{LiO}_{2}$ is incline to dissolving in the electrolyte, leading to the formation of $\operatorname{Li}_{2}0_{2}$ via the chemical disproportionation reaction $(2\mathrm{LiO}_{2}$ (s $_{\mathrm{ol)}}\rightarrow\mathrm{Li}_{2}{\ensuremath{0_{2}}}+{\ensuremath{0_{2}}})$ on pristine MOF electrode. Accordingly, discretely distributed $\mathrm{Li}_{2}{\sf O}_{2}$ grains can be generated according to this solution-mediated pathway due to the sparse nucleation sites on the surface of the pristine MOF. As a consequence, large-sized $\mathrm{Li}_{2}{\sf O}_{2}$ can be discretely and loosely deposited on the active sites, resulting in the poor contact between $\operatorname{Li}_{2}0_{2}$ and active sites. During the charge process, trapped in inherent insulating properties of well-crystallized $\operatorname{Li}_{2}0_{2}$ and their poor contact with electrode surface, the utilization of the sparse active sites is inefficient, and thus, causing terrible electrochemical performance of the pristine MOF based LOBs, including superhigh charge overpotential and low round-trip efficiency. In stark contrast, the crucial intermediate $\mathrm{LiO}_{2}$ is imprisoned around plentiful active sites on the surface of $2\%$ -TS MOF due to the considerably strong interaction between $\mathrm{LiO}_{2}$ and active sites. Thus, the immobilized $\mathrm{LiO}_{2}$ undergoes a second electron reduction reaction $\left(\mathrm{LiO}_{2(\mathrm{ads)}}+\mathrm{e}^{-}+\mathrm{Li}^{+}\rightarrow\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}\right)$ on the electrode surface to form $\operatorname{Li}_{2}0_{2}$ . Encouraged by the abundant nucleation sites, uniformly distributed amorphous $\operatorname{Li}_{2}0_{2}$ can be obtained via the surface-adsorption pathway. Consequently, closely packed amorphous $\operatorname{Li}_{2}0_{2}$ film deposits intimately on the surface of $2\%$ -TS MOF, resulting in an ultra-low contact resistance at the interface between $\operatorname{Li}_{2}0_{2}$ and the electrode. During the charge process, the active sites are sufficiently utilized due to the intimate contact interface, leading to the favorable decomposition of the amorphous $\operatorname{Li}_{2}0_{2}$ on the surface of $2\%$ -TS MOF electrode.
## 4. Conclusion
In summary, it is confirmed that the catalytic activity of MOFs towards oxygen redox reactions in LOBs can be improved by tailoring the degree of intrinsic tensile strain. After the introduction of tensile strain in MOFs, their adsorption capability towards intermediate $\mathrm{LiO}_{2}$ can be enhanced, eventually leading to the desired nucleation and growth pathway of $\operatorname{Li}_{2}0_{2}$ . As a result, amorphous film-like $\operatorname{Li}_{2}0_{2}$ uniformly and closely distributes on the surface of $2\%$ -TS MOF during discharge process, which is remarkably different from the well-crystallized large-size $\operatorname{Li}_{2}0_{2}$ formed on the surface of the pristine MOF. Benefiting from the intimate contact between discharged product and electrode surface and the advantage of facile decomposition of the amorphous $\mathrm{Li}_{2}0_{2}$ , the large discharge capacity ( $.12317.4\mathrm{~mAh~g}^{-1}$ ), ultralow overpotential (0.61 V) and extended cyclability (357 cycles) are achieved. This work reveals the crucial role of tensile strain in regulating the growth pathway of $\operatorname{Li}_{2}0_{2}$ . This design philosophy of electrocatalyst could be spread to other energy storge field.
